---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const scaleCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Communication paths grow as n(n−1)/2', note: 'Doubling headcount more than doubles coordination load. At 10 people, everyone knows everything; at 100, that is impossible. Structure replaces hallway — deliberately or badly.' },
    { math: '2 · Structure follows strategy (Chandler)', note: 'Organise around what the strategy needs: function (efficiency at one thing), division (accountability per market), matrix (both, at coordination cost). Each redesign is a bet about what matters now.' },
    { math: '3 · Processes replace heroics', note: 'What founders did by intuition must become documented process: hiring rubrics, incident playbooks, decision rights (who decides what, consult whom — RAPID/DACI). Process is how quality survives headcount.' },
    { math: '4 · Culture scales through rituals', note: 'Culture degrades by default as new people arrive. Rituals (demos, post-mortems, written decisions) transmit it; promotion patterns cement it. Who gets promoted IS the culture announcement.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/business/expert/scaling-and-organisational-design">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Scaling a company is <strong>redesigning it repeatedly without stopping the
    machinery</strong>. The 10-person startup's superpower (everyone talks to everyone) becomes
    the 200-person company's failure mode. Organisational design — structure, process, culture —
    is the deliberate replacement of hallway conversation with systems that scale.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="coordination maths, structure, process, culture transmission" widget="equationStep" config={scaleCfg} />

  <h2>The scaling playbooks, compared</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Reorganisation is expensive:</strong> reorgs cost 6–12 months of productivity each. Frequent small adjustments beat rare reorganisations — and sometimes the answer is fewer changes of structure, more clarity of decision rights.</li>
    <li><strong>Managers of managers:</strong> the hardest promotion in a company is the first manager-of-managers role — the skills (delegation through others, organisational design) are genuinely different. Skip it and you get a pyramid of individual contributors with titles.</li>
    <li><strong>Decision hygiene scales best of all:</strong> written decisions with owners, dates and reasoning (Amazon's 6-page memos, Stripe's writing culture) — documentation compounds while meetings don't.</li>
    <li><strong>Know when to stop scaling:</strong> not every business should be 10,000 people — the "small giants" evidence (Bo Burlingham) shows profitability and craft survive at deliberate scale ceilings. Growth is a choice with costs, not a law.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Chandler, Strategy and Structure (1962); Meaghan Johnson/RAPID framework (Bain); Burlingham, Small Giants; Sahil Lavingia's "reflecting on my failure" (Gumroad, 2019).</p>
</TopicPage>
